### 6. PR #257 -- CPU batch=1 fanout cap + per-SKU bandwidth (closes #175)

Landed 2026-05-26 as a same-session continuation. Two compounding issues let a
many-core CPU overstate batch=1 throughput.

- Fix #1 (concurrency cap): a single operator only fans out across
  `min(num_cores, total_macs / MIN_MACS_PER_CORE)` cores (64K MACs/core floor).
  `RooflineAnalyzer._cpu_concurrency_scale` (CPU-gated) scales the compute
  ceiling by effective_cores/num_cores; the CPUMapper allocation caps
  cores_allocated the same way. Verified: batch=1 Linear(2048,2048) -> 64/192 on
  AmpereOne (matvec compute_time 0.84us -> 2.5us).
- Fix #2 (bandwidth): peak_bandwidth_gbps threaded through
  cpu_arm_resource_model; AmpereOne 192/128 -> 332.8 GB/s datasheet (was 80).

Diagnostic finding: in the current code (post-#172 LLC stationarity + per-op
overhead) the catalog batch=1 matvec on AmpereOne-192 is OVERHEAD-bound, not
compute-bound -- weights are LLC-resident (~0.08us) and a ~6.5us/op launch
overhead dominates. So the headline 71K inf/s barely moves; the issue's "192x
compute fanout" premise predated those models. Per maintainer decision, shipped
the correct cap + bandwidth fix + a cap-level regression test (not an overhead/
LLC-bandwidth rework). 1781 tests green; AmpereOne validations unchanged (no
regression, confirmed identical pre/post).

## What's next

- KPU + CPU-mapper issue thread fully closed. No immediate follow-ups; the
